  nonce: ffee  # ffee
  data:  # ffee
    kind: invariant  # ffee
    title: "A registry populated by import side effect must be restorable after a clear; a bare re-import is a no-op and silently reports success"  # ffee
    status: violated  # ffee
    priority: 2  # ffee
    parent:  # ffee
    tags:  # ffee
    - taint  # ffee
    - testing  # ffee
    isbefore: []  # ffee
    duplicate_of: []  # ffee
    not_duplicate_of: []  # ffee
    pr_ref:  # ffee
    description: "FIXED IN THE SAME PR THAT FOUND IT. Filed anyway because the CLASS is broader than\nthe instance and the instance was already known-and-worked-around once.\n\nTHE DEFECT. dataflow_scope.ensure_def_use_extractors_registered() restores the\ndef/use extractor registry by IMPORTING the four extractor modules for their\ndecorator side effect. A bare import is a no-op once the module is in sys.modules,\nso after cfg.clear_def_use_extractors() the re-import restored NOTHING while the\nfunction still returned True. Every language then reported dataflow_capable=False,\nand a taint claim over JavaScript came back structural/approximate instead of\nddg/precise.\n\nHOW IT PRESENTED, which is the expensive part. Two test_taint_recall_corpus tests\nfailed in a full-suite run, passed at every narrower scope (core alone; core +\nmainstream + common, 17,900 tests), and passed when re-run in isolation. A green\nsuite therefore carried no information about these assertions. It reproduced across\ntwo consecutive full runs, so it is order-dependent rather than random: it needs\ntest_cfg.py (which clears the registry five times) or test_py_def_use.py to land in\nthe same xdist worker, ahead of the recall corpus.\n\nALREADY KNOWN, ALREADY WORKED AROUND, LOCALLY AND ONCE.\ntest_ddg_build.py::test_python_still_produces_edges carries a hand-rolled\nimportlib.reload guard whose docstring states the mechanism exactly: \"test_cfg.py\ncalls clear_def_use_extractors(); a bare import of the extractor module is a no-op\nonce it is already in sys.modules, so the registration would NOT be restored and\nthis floor would fail\". One test defended itself; every other consumer -- including\nproduction's own coverage table -- was unguarded. That is the CLASS: a per-site\nworkaround for a shared defect, with no mechanism making the other sites do it.\n\nTHE FIRST FIX WAS WRONG AND ITS TESTS PASSED. It keyed the reload on \"is the\nregistry EMPTY\", reasoning that clear_def_use_extractors is all-or-nothing and that\na list of expected languages here would be a second home for a fact the decorators\nhold. Both premises are true and the conclusion was still wrong: test_py_def_use.py\nclears the registry and then reloads ONLY ITS OWN module, leaving it POPULATED BUT\nINCOMPLETE. An emptiness check sees health; javascript is still gone. Verified\ndirectly (clear -> reload py_def_use -> ensure() -> javascript still absent) rather\nthan inferred, after a full-suite run showed the same two failures unchanged.\n\nTHE SHIPPED FIX learns the expected language set from the first successful\nregistration in the process and reloads whenever the live registry stops being a\nsuperset of it. No hardcoded language list, and it repairs both the full-clear and\nthe partial-restore cases. Full suite after: 22,255 passed / 0 failed / 100%\ncoverage, against 2 failed before.\n\nWHY THIS IS FILED RATHER THAN JUST FIXED -- THE UNCLOSED PART:\n 1. ddg_build.clear_ddg_languages() has the SAME shape (_DDG_LANGUAGES is populated\n    by import side effect and cleared by tests) and is NOT guarded. It did not cause\n    the observed failure -- python passed, so the ddg_spec bit was intact -- but the\n    mechanism is identical and nothing prevents it.\n 2. cfg.clear_cfg_mapping_cache() and the _MAPPING_CACHE were CHECKED AND CLEARED of\n    suspicion: the cache key includes search_dir, so a test using a temp directory\n    cannot poison the default entry. Recorded so the next investigator does not\n    re-derive it.\n 3. No test asserts that a test-only mutator (clear_*) leaves the process in a\n    recoverable state. The general form -- \"a registry populated by import side\n    effect cannot be restored by importing again\" -- applies to any future registry\n    added on this pattern, and there is no gate that would catch the next one."  # ffee
    fields:  # ffee
      statement: "Any registry populated as an import side effect must expose a restore path that works after the registry is cleared. Re-importing an already-imported module does not re-run its decorators, so a restore built on import alone returns success while restoring nothing."  # ffee
      root_cause: "dataflow_scope.ensure_def_use_extractors_registered restored the def/use registry by importing four modules; sys.modules caching made that a no-op after cfg.clear_def_use_extractors(). ddg_build.clear_ddg_languages() has the identical shape and is still unguarded."  # ffee
